#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""cst-toolchains: the caches of the developer toolchains (cargo and uv,
later go, npm, maven) and the toolchain settings CST writes. It is the program behind
`cst toolchains` (toolchains-setup.sh), and the hourly user timer runs a copy
of it (~/.local/lib/cst/cst-toolchains). Always as the user, never as root.

Commands:
  status [--brief]               per target: what and where, size, policy,
                                 last prune; the timer; the installed rustup
                                 toolchains (a report: which are pinned or named
                                 in build files); the toolchain settings
  prune plan  [TARGET...]        what prune apply would delete, and why
  prune apply [TARGET...]        delete now (the timer runs this, no TARGET)
  auto on [--unless-off]         install this program and the user timer
                                 cst-toolchains-prune.timer (hourly) and start it
                                 (--unless-off: not if `auto off` was run;
                                 `cst btrfs snapshots setup` uses it)
  auto off                       stop the timer, remove what `auto on` installed,
                                 and remember the choice (~/.config/cst/toolchains-auto.off)
  config plan | apply [--lean-debuginfo | --no-lean-debuginfo] [--sccache | --no-sccache]
                                 the toolchain settings in your home: cargo's
                                 build-dir in ~/.cargo/config.toml, uv's
                                 link-mode in ~/.config/uv/uv.toml, the
                                 opt-in lean debug info (RUST_LEAN_DEBUGINFO)
                                 and the opt-in sccache (RUST_SCCACHE; only
                                 --no-sccache removes it again)
  help
Every command takes --home DIR (default: your home).

TARGET is LANG:CACHE (rust:build) or LANG (all targets of that language).
Without TARGET, prune runs every target enabled in the settings. A target
named on the command line runs even when it is disabled (disabled = left out
of the timer and of the default selection).

Targets:
  rust:build     cargo's intermediate build files, ~/.cache/cargo-build/XX/YYYYYYYYYYYYYY
                 (one folder per workspace path; cargo never deletes them)
  python:uv      `uv cache prune` on ~/.cache/uv (only entries uv no longer uses)
  Planned, not implemented yet: rust:registry, go:build (both tools already
  clean these by themselves), go:mod, node:npm, jvm:maven.

Settings: KEY=value lines; the defaults are in toolchains.conf next to this
program (toolchains/toolchains.conf in CST), yours in
~/.config/cst/toolchains.conf win. Keys are <LANG>_<CACHE>_<SETTING>:
  RUST_BUILD_ENABLED=yes     the timer and a bare `prune` run it (yes or no)
  RUST_BUILD_MAX_IDLE=48h    delete a folder whose newest activity is older
  RUST_BUILD_MAX_TOTAL=150G  then, while the total is above this, delete the
                             least recently active folders first (0 or off: no cap)
  RUST_BUILD_MIN_KEEP=2h     never delete a folder active more recently than
                             this (also not for the cap, orphans, incremental);
                             at least 15m
  RUST_BUILD_ORPHANS=yes     delete a folder no workspace claims any more
  RUST_BUILD_INCREMENTAL_IDLE=6h  in a folder idle longer than this, delete
                             only <profile>/incremental (0 or off: never)
  PYTHON_UV_ENABLED=yes      uv cache prune ...
  PYTHON_UV_INTERVAL=1d      ... at most once per this (0: every run)
  RUST_LEAN_DEBUGINFO=no     yes: `config apply` writes lean debug info for
                             dev builds into ~/.cargo/config.toml
  RUST_SCCACHE=no            yes: `config apply` installs
                             ~/.local/libexec/cst/rustc-wrapper and names it
                             as [build] rustc-wrapper (rustc through sccache)
  RUST_SCCACHE_CACHE_SIZE=25G  sccache's size cap for ~/.cache/sccache
Durations: a number with s, m, h, d or w. Sizes: a number with K, M, G or T
(binary: G = GiB; "GiB", "GB" and "G" all mean 2^30 bytes); the unit is
required (a bare 150 is an error, not 150 bytes). An unknown key or a bad
value stops the run: nothing is deleted. Sizes are what `du` shows: the
files' size before btrfs compression (zstd usually stores less, and RAID1
stores that twice).

rust:build in detail. `cst toolchains config apply` (also run by `cst btrfs
snapshots setup`) writes into ~/.cargo/config.toml:

    [build]
    build-dir = "<home>/.cache/cargo-build/{workspace-path-hash}"

so every cargo workspace path (every git worktree, every agent checkout) gets
its own folder of intermediate build files, <root>/XX/YYYYYYYYYYYYYY (cargo's
16-hex-digit hash of the manifest path, split 2 + 14). Cargo never deletes
them (`cargo clean gc` does not cover build-dir).

Activity of a folder = the newest modification time (mtime) of anything in
it: every entry is visited anyway to measure the size, so the newest mtime
costs nothing more. Each build that compiles something creates or rewrites
files (rustc output in deps/, incremental session folders, fingerprints), so
the newest mtime is the last build that did work. A no-op build changes
nothing and does not count (deleting then only costs one rebuild). mtimes
more than 5 minutes in the future are ignored (a wrong clock or a copied
file); a folder with nothing else counts as active now.

Safety: only folders <root>/XX/YYYYYYYYYYYYYY (lowercase hex) are touched;
anything else in the root is reported and left alone; symlinks are refused
and never followed; entries on another filesystem are not counted and not
deleted (rm --one-file-system); a folder is skipped while a process of the
user has its working directory, executable, an open file or a mapped file
in it (/proc; toolbox containers share the host's process list), or while
cargo's lock (<profile>/.cargo-build-lock, <triple>/<profile>/... and the
older .cargo-lock) is held, or while a running cargo (cargo, cargo-nextest,
...) uses it: cargo holds no lock while tests run, so each running cargo is
asked which build folder it uses (`cargo metadata --no-deps --offline` in
its folder, with its own cargo and settings); if that cannot be found out,
nothing is deleted while it runs. A non-blocking flock is taken on each lock
first and held while the users are checked, the folder is measured again
(a build may have written there since the run started: if the rule that
chose the action no longer holds, it is kept and planned again) and the
folder is renamed away (atomic); then the renamed folder is deleted. A cargo that was waiting for
the lock then rebuilds from scratch into a new folder at the same path. If
build-dir in ~/.cargo/config.toml is not exactly the line CST writes,
nothing is done.

Orphans. Each folder is named after a workspace's Cargo.toml path. Every
git repository under ~/Workspace (6 levels deep; not node_modules, target,
hidden or CACHEDIR.TAG folders) is listed with `git worktree list
--porcelain` (also linked worktrees elsewhere); each Cargo.toml in those
worktrees (a workspace answers for its members) is asked `cargo metadata
--offline --no-deps --format-version 1` (read only, idle priority, 60 s each,
600 s in all; RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0). Its build_directory field names the
folder the workspace claims. A folder idle longer than MIN_KEEP that nobody
claims is deleted ("orphan: no workspace claims it"), with the same lock and
in-use checks. If a git or cargo call fails or times out, or nothing is
claimed at all, no folder counts as an orphan in that run (plan and the
journal say why). A crate that cargo refuses to build where it is ("believes
it's in a workspace when it's not") claims nothing and is not a failure.

Incremental. In a folder idle longer than INCREMENTAL_IDLE, not in use and
not locked, only the incremental folders (<profile>/incremental and
<triple>/<profile>/incremental) are deleted: they are most of a folder's size
and only speed up rebuilding the workspace's own crates. Their parent's
mtime is put back, so the folder's last activity does not move.

python:uv. `uv cache prune --cache-dir ~/.cache/uv` (never --force), with uv
from PATH (refused when absent; `auto on` checks the user manager's PATH,
which the timer uses). uv holds a shared lock on <cache>/.lock while any uv
command runs; if it is held the run is skipped, and uv itself waits at most
60 s (UV_LOCK_TIMEOUT) for a uv that starts meanwhile (only uv's timeout
message counts as that; any other error is a failure).

Time budget. One `prune apply` keeps to 25 minutes (the service is stopped
at 30): targets with a fixed bound (python:uv) run first; rust:build starts
no deletion with less than 2 minutes left and stops an rm at the end (the
next run deletes the leftover).

In a toolbox this program runs itself on the host (flatpak-spawn --host):
only the host sees the processes of every container. Exit status: 0 fine
(also "nothing to do"), 1 refused or failed, 2 usage.
"""

